{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.4,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["T4", "T6"],
      "pins": ["T4.3", "T6.3"],
      "nets": ["ATO valve low-side drain node (V2 / connector P1, \"NET0067\")", "+24V input path (\"Reverse current protection\")"],
      "observed": "The ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch is drawn as T4, an AO3400A (SOT-23, 30 V / 5.7 A class) fed from the pre-driver T2 (BT3904) through R53 (100 Ω). The 8-DFN AON6407, which the specification calls out as the ATO-valve switch, instead appears in the 24 V input path, annotated \"Reverse current protection\" (T6).",
      "expected": "Per the specification the AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) is the ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch, and the AO3400A is the single small device of the r2 discrete set (spec §5 lists T3 = AON6407 and T5 = AO3400A).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) | ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "r2 drops to a smaller discrete-switch set (T1/T4 BT3904 ×2, T2 IRLR2905, T3 AON6407, T5 AO3400A ×1)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "ATO Valve block: \"T4  AO3400A\" driven from \"T2 BT3904\"; Power Train: \"Reverse current protection  T6  AON6407\""}
      ],
      "impact": "The two N-channel MOSFETs are placed in each other's positions (sourcing/placement mix-up). The valve still switches, but through the smaller SOT-23 device with roughly an order of magnitude higher Rds(on) and far less thermal/power margin than the intended DFN part.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R25"],
      "pins": [],
      "nets": ["float-switch node pulled to GND (FLOAT_HIGH)"],
      "observed": "R25, the pull-down resistor on the FLOAT_HIGH float-switch input in the \"Water Level Sensor/Switches\" block, is drawn as 1.0 kΩ (the companion pull-up R30 is 10 kΩ).",
      "expected": "10 kΩ. The specification quotes the reference netlist showing the high-level float pull-down as 10 kΩ: \"R9 GND FLOAT_HIGH 10k\".",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "with 10 kΩ/4.7 kΩ pull resistors visible in the .cir file: R6 FLOAT_LOW +3V3 10k, R9 GND FLOAT_HIGH 10k"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Safety Switches block: \"R30 10k\" to +3V3 and \"R25 1.0k\" to GND"}
      ],
      "impact": "Ten times the standing current is forced through the float reed switch and its wiring; the digitised logic level itself is unaffected, so the fault is easily missed in functional test.",
      "confidence": 0.3
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Most of the drawing matches the specification closely (I2C/EZO pin map, three TMC2209 drivers with 110 mΩ sense resistors, 2.2 kΩ I2C pull-ups, ADM3260 isolated pH/EC channels, DNP environmental sensors and the unwired LiDAR module), so I have only low-confidence findings: the AON6407/AO3400A MOSFET pair appears to be swapped between the ATO-valve switch and the 24 V input protection, and the FLOAT_HIGH pull-down R25 is 1.0 kΩ instead of the specified 10 kΩ."
}

